## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why This Breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — the runtime no longer supports preemptive thread termination. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation** via `CancellationToken`.

---

### Pattern Classification

| Original Code | Pattern | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| `while (_running)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Force stop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `GetNextItem()` (blocking) | Blocking call interruption | Pass token to unblock |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Equivalent to the old ThreadAbortException handler
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop cleanly (replaces ResetAbort + loop exit)
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after timeout, there's nothing equivalent to Abort().
        // The thread must cooperate. Log a warning if needed.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

The blocking `GetNextItem()` **must** accept a `CancellationToken` — otherwise cancellation stalls until it returns:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework)
private WorkItem GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks indefinitely
}

// After (.NET 8) — using BlockingCollection<T>
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
}
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---|---|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation only triggers at the next checkpoint. If `ProcessItem()` is long-running, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside it. |
| **No force-kill equivalent** | If code truly cannot cooperate, you must move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`. |
| **Dispose the CTS** | `CancellationTokenSource` leaks timers if not disposed. |
| **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the top-level loop; let it propagate elsewhere. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` ignores tokens** | Replace `Thread.Sleep(ms)` with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` if you need cancellable waits. |

---

### If `ProcessItem` Is Long-Running

Add checkpoints inside the work:

```csharp
private void ProcessItem(WorkItem item, CancellationToken token)
{
    Step1(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint

    Step2(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint

    Step3(item);
}
```

This ensures the thread responds to cancellation within a bounded time, replicating the "interruptibility" that `Thread.Abort` previously provided — but safely and deterministically.
